, -0.01824951171875, 0.0033054351806640625, -0.03363037109375, -0.005523681640625, 0.04876708984375, 0.0024967193603515625, -0.00893402099609375, 0.01363372802734375, 0.022003173828125, 0.01366424560546875, 0.00963592529296875, -0.0282745361328125, -0.034515380859375, 0.0191497802734375, 0.01331329345703125, 0.01099395751953125, 0.007320404052734375, -0.0016994476318359375, -0.0009255409240722656, 0.0084228515625, -0.006259918212890625, 0.01861572265625, 0.01319122314453125, 0.0013799667358398438, -0.01218414306640625, 0.0013799667358398438, -0.0234222412109375, -0.0020999908447265625, -0.0022430419921875, 0.0128631591796875, 0.04315185546875, 0.0225982666015625, -0.016143798828125, 0.0019397735595703125, 0.033599853515625, -0.00260162353515625, 0.0631103515625, -0.04974365234375, 0.03265380859375, 0.005474090576171875, -0.033294677734375, -0.005474090576171875, -0.027862548828125, 0.004077911376953125, 0.0131072998046875, -0.040069580078125, 0.0009775161743164062, -0.007965087890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are essential for achieving low-carbon heat supply, but they face significant challenges due to increasingly heterogeneous and time-varying renewable sources, the integration of thermal storage, and complex meshed topologies. This paper addresses the global optimization of district heating network operations over multiple time steps, using a stationary yet realistic nonlinear network model. To enhance the efficiency of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for single time-step optimization, several novel methodological improvements are introduced: exclusion of cyclic flows, exploitation of symmetry between supply and return networks, reduction of temperature mixing constraints, and the development of new primal heuristics and branching rules. These methods are evaluated on both synthetic and real-world benchmark networks that include cycles and multiple suppliers. On synthetic benchmarks, the proposed techniques more than double the number of solved instances and reduce runtime by over 50%. For real-world benchmarks, the algorithm produces solutions with guaranteed quality within reasonable computation times. For multi-step problems coupled by thermal storage, a time decomposition approach is explored. Under practically reasonable assumptions, this approach is proven to yield optimal solutions. In a small example network, the decomposition computes optimal solutions in under a second, whereas solving the full time-coupled problem directly is infeasible within an hour."}}